{
  "about": [
    "Pregenerated parties for play or playtest, so a table with no characters to hand can still run an adventure.",
    "Each member names a class, species, background, `gear` list, persona, and `rules` brief for the harness to give that seat and the game master. The member also has a `sheet` — an engine combatant spec accepted as-is by encounter.create and adventure.encounter. Only `sheet` reaches the engine: it refuses unknown spec keys, so identity, briefing, temperament, voice, and gear may never be folded into it.",
    "The engine models no character building: labels never derive mechanics. Final abilities, saves, background skill totals, spell resources, attacks, and supported features are written directly in `sheet`; `rules` names their source and names features the engine only partly supports or cannot represent.",
    "The spell lists are streamlined executable combat loadouts, not full class prepared-spell lists. Every positive slot level has a native spell, while each Magic Initiate background names both chosen cantrips and its level-1 spell; non-executable utility magic and the feat's independent free cast stay explicit in `rules`. Ilma's Sacred Flame and Doran's Fire Bolt remain at-will cantrips, and the engine's lack of character levels means their level-5 damage does not scale.",
    "Healing is deliberately scarce: one Cure Wounds caster and a potion or two, because a party that cannot be worn down measures nothing about an adventuring day. Potions are a Bonus Action, so carrying one costs a character nothing until it is drunk.",
    "A ranged attack declares `normal_range` and `long_range` in feet, and those two keys are the only ones the engine reads — an attack that names neither is built with a maximum range of 0 feet and refused at every distance. The bow, crossbow and javelin also name the `ammunition` they spend, and the sheet stocks it: an empty quiver refuses the shot before it is rolled, and the crossbow's `loading` allows one shot per turn.",
    "`gear` is fiction, not inventory. The engine's `items` map holds only what it can resolve — a potion it defines, or ammunition an attack spends — so rope, torches and a healer's kit live outside the sheet, where the game master adjudicates them. A player who asks to use one gets a ruling, never a roll.",
    "This is our own authored content, not transcribed from any source. The spell and item names refer to records in the bundled SRD 5.2.1 slice, which carries its own provenance."
  ],
